MP-03 — Webhook Verification & HMAC

Technology learned:
HMAC-based webhook authentication/verification using Python.

Why I chose/was assigned this technology:
I had little/no prior practical experience implementing webhook
verification and had not previously built an authenticated webhook
endpoint.

What I learned:
A webhook is an HTTP request sent by one system to notify another
system that an event has occurred. The receiving system should not
blindly trust the request. HMAC(in python) can be used to verify that the sender
knows a shared secret.

The sender generates an HMAC signature from the request body and
shared secret. The receiver independently generates the expected
signature using the same secret and compares the two signatures.

Mini-prototype:
I first implemented HMAC signature generation and verification in
webhook_test.py without FastAPI.

Tests:
1. Matching generated and received signatures → True.
2. Deliberately incorrect signature → False.

Implementation:
I then implemented a FastAPI POST endpoint:

POST /webhook/print-complete

The endpoint:
- reads the incoming request body
- reads the X-Signature header
- generates the expected HMAC-SHA256 signature
- compares the received and expected signatures
- accepts valid signatures
- rejects invalid signatures
- rejects requests with no signature

I created a fake printer program to send an actual HTTP POST request
to the FastAPI webhook.

Troubleshooting:
The initial webhook request returned HTTP 500. I investigated the
Uvicorn server output and identified the problem.

The webhook signature initially failed verification because the shared
secret used by the backend and fake printer did not match exactly.
The backend used "mys-super-secret" while the fake printer used
"my-super-secret". Since HMAC depends on the exact secret and message,
the signatures were different.

I corrected the shared secret so both systems used the same value.

Final verification:
- Correct signature → accepted
- Incorrect signature → rejected
- Missing signature → rejected

Key takeaway:
The webhook is not inherently authenticated simply because it is an
HTTP endpoint. Authentication/verification has to be implemented.
HMAC allows the receiving server to verify that the sender knows the
shared secret without sending the secret itself in the request.
